-- title:
--   Computationally connected subsystems
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be a gate set on $\mathcal H=\bigotimes_{i}\mathbb C^{d_i}$. Two subsystems $a\ne b$ are **computationally connected** for $G$ if every operator $V_{ab}$ with
--   $$
--   V\in\mathrm{SU}(d_a d_b)
--   $$
--   acting on the factors $a,b$ (and as the identity elsewhere) is densely generated by $G$, that is, lies in the closure $\overline{\langle G\rangle}$ of the set of finite products of gates.
--
--   The book says that $a$ and $b$ are computationally connected "if operators on the combined Hilbert space $\mathcal H_a\otimes\mathcal H_b$ densely generate $\mathrm{SU}(d_ad_b)$". The condition here asks only that $\mathrm{SU}(d_ad_b)$ on the pair be approximable by products of any gates of $G$, not necessarily of those acting on $a,b$ alone; the book's condition implies it.
--
--   These are the edges of the graph of Lemma 4.3.2.
-- source:
--   Bacon, Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1, Lemma 4.3.2, p.52 (PDF p.69)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_Universality_denseGen
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_Universality_embedPair

namespace BaconThesis.Universality

/-- **Computationally connected subsystems.**
Bacon, *Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers*, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1,
§4.3.1, Lemma 4.3.2, p.52 (PDF p.69): "We say that two subsystems H_a and H_b of dimensions d_a and
d_b are computationally connected if operators on the combined Hilbert space H_a ⊗ H_b densely
generate SU(d_a d_b)."

For a gate set `G` on `⊗_i ℂ^{d_i}`, two distinct subsystems `a ≠ b` are computationally connected
when every `V ⊗ I` with `V ∈ SU(d_a d_b)` acting on the factors `a, b` lies in the set densely
generated by `G`.

**Formalization Note.** The book asks that the gates acting on `H_a ⊗ H_b` alone densely generate
`SU(d_a d_b)`; here every element of `SU(d_a d_b) ⊗ I` need only be approximable by products of
gates from all of `G`. This condition is implied by the book's, so the lemma that assumes it is
more general. -/
def CompConnected {n : ℕ} (d : Fin n → ℕ)
    (G : Set (Matrix.unitaryGroup ((i : Fin n) → Fin (d i)) ℂ)) (a b : Fin n) : Prop :=
  a ≠ b ∧ ∀ V ∈ Matrix.specialUnitaryGroup (Fin (d a) × Fin (d b)) ℂ,
    embedPair d a b V ∈ denseGen G

end BaconThesis.Universality


